  "content": {
    "title": "Tennessee basketball stifles Miami (Ohio) in NCAA Tournament opening win",
    "text": "Updated March 20, 2026, 10:24 p.m. ETPHILADELPHIA − Tennessee basketball opened the Men's NCAA Tournament without much drama.The No. 6 seed Vols (23-11) avoided an upset by crushing No. 11 Miami of Ohio (32-2) in the first round of March Madness, 78-56, at Xfinity Mobile Arena on March 20.Tennessee will play No. 3 Virginia (30-5) in the second round on March 22. The winner will advance to the Sweet 16 in Chicago as a part of the Midwest Region.Ja'Kobi Gillespie tied a Tennessee record in the NCAA Tournament with six 3-pointers on his way to 29 points. Miami (Ohio) couldn't find any answers for the senior guard, while the Vols defense held the RedHawks to their lowest-scoring output of the season.J.P Estrella finished with 14 points and 10 rebounds, while Felix Okpara added 12 points and five rebounds. Star freshman Nate Ament was scoreless on 0-of-3 shooting.Tennessee basketball vs Miami (Ohio) live score Tennessee growing lead over Miami (Ohio)The Vols haven't been quite as efficient on the offensive end in the second half, shooting 4-of-10 from the field, but it doesn't matter. Their defense has only allowed four points to the RedHawks through eight minutes. Tennessee has its largest lead of the game, 62-36 with 11:20 remaining. Ja'Kobi Gillespie on pace for Vols historyGillespie already has 22 points at the half against Miami (Ohio). The only other Vols players to score 30 or more points in the NCAA Tournament are Ernie Grunfeld and Dalton Knecht. Grunfeld had 30 points in 1976, while Knecht holds the record with a 37-point performance in Tennessee's Elite Eight loss to Purdue in 2024. Ethan Burg saves Clarence Massamba minutes with buzzer 3After Ja'Kobi Gillespie drilled his fifth 3-pointer, Rick Barnes turned to depth freshman guard Clarence Massamba to give Gillespie a break in the final minute. Massamba soon turned the ball over, leading to a Miami (Ohio) layup. Ethan Burg responded with a last-second 3 that bounced around every area of the rim before dropping in. Tennessee leads 51-32 at the half. Ja'Kobi Gillespie nearly outdueling Miami (Ohio)With his fourth 3-pointer of the first half, Ja'Kobi Gillespie is up to 19 points. Miami (Ohio) has 23 points as a team. He also has four assists and two steals. Airballs mounting for RedHawks while Vols offense humsMiami (Ohio) is now up to four airballs and hasn't hit a 3-pointer for nearly nine minutes of game time. The Vols' offense, meanwhile, is clicking at its highest rate of the season. Ja'Kobi Gillespie is 5-of-6 and tossing lobs to J.P. Estrella and Felix Okpara. An Ethan Burg 3-pointer put Tennessee up 31-17 and forced the RedHawks to call a timeout. Ja'Kobi Gillespie taking over while Miami (Ohio) coolsJa'Kobi Gillespie has 10 of Tennessee's 23 points on 4-of-4 shooting. He's knocked down both of his 3-pointers, while Miami (Ohio)'s offense slowed down. The RedHawks have now airballed three 3-poitners and are 6-of-14 from the field compared to the Vols' 9-of-13 shooting. Tennessee leads 23-15. Miami (Ohio) ignoring Tennessee's defenseThe RedHawks continued their hot shooting performance from their win over SMU. They're 3-of-4 from 3-point range. The Vols are contesting each shot, but without much affect. Tennessee has countered with eight points in the paint, but Miami (Ohio) still leads 13-10. Tipoff clock for Tennessee basketball vs Miami (Ohio)The No. 6 Vols and No. 11 RedHawks tipoff at 4:25 p.m. The arena clock is at nine minutes and counting.What time does Tennessee basketball vs Miami (Ohio) start?Date: Friday, March 20Time: 4:25 p.m. ETWhere: Xfinity Mobile ArenaWhat channel is Tennessee basketball vs Miami (Ohio) on today?TV Channel: TBSLive stream: Sling TVTennessee basketball betting lineTennessee is favored by 12.5 points over Miami (Ohio) on BetMGM.Tennessee basketball March Madness historyThe Vols have made 28 NCAA Tournament appearances with an overall 31-28 record. They have qualified for March Madness eight seasons in a row, and have made the Sweet 16 the last three years.Tennessee has been to the Elite Eight three times: once under Bruce Pearl in 2010, and twice under Rick Barnes in 2024 and 2025. It has never been to the Final Four.Nate Ament 2026 NBA Draft projectionHeading into the NCAA Tournament, Ament's NBA Draft projection has settled around the back half of the lottery. Draft analysts were initially concerned with Ament's slow start, but his emergence throughout SEC play points to a higher professional ceiling.USA TODAY's Bryan Kalbrosky has the lowest current projection on Ament, slotting him at No. 11.The Athletic, ESPN and CBS have the freshman going off the board at No. 9.Tennessee basketball 2025-26 scheduleNov. 3: Tennessee 76, Mercer 61Nov. 8: Tennessee 95, Northern Kentucky 56Nov. 12: Tennessee 99, North Florida 66Nov. 17: Tennessee 91, Rice 66Nov. 20: Tennessee 89, Tennessee State 60Nov. 24: Tennessee 85, Rutgers 60Nov. 25: Tennessee 76, Houston 73Nov. 26: Kansas 81, Tennessee 76Dec. 2: Syracuse 62, Tennessee 60Dec. 6: Illinois 75, Tennessee 62Dec. 16: Tennessee 83, Louisville 62Dec. 21: Tennessee 94, Gardner-Webb 52Dec. 30: Tennessee 105, South Carolina State 54Jan. 3: Arkansas 86, Tennessee 75Jan. 6: Tennessee 85, Texas 71Jan. 10: Florida 91, Tennessee 67Jan. 13: Tennessee 87, Texas A&M 82 (2OT)Jan 17: Kentucky 80, Tennessee 78Jan. 24: Tennessee 79, Alabama 73Jan. 27: Tennessee 86, Georgia 85 (OT)Jan. 31: Tennessee 77, Auburn 69Feb. 3: Tennessee 84, Ole Miss 66Feb 7: Kentucky 74, Tennessee 71Feb. 11: Tennessee 73, Mississippi State 64Feb 14: Tennessee 73, LSU 63Feb. 18: Tennessee 89, Oklahoma 66Feb 21: Tennessee 69, Vanderbilt 65Feb. 24: Missouri 73, Tennessee 69Feb. 28: Alabama 71, Tennessee 69March 3: Tennessee 78, South Carolina 59March 7: Vanderbilt 86, Tennessee 82March 12: Tennessee 72, Auburn 62March 13: Vanderbilt 75, Tennessee 68Wynton Jackson covers high school sports for Knox News. Email: wynton.jackson@knoxnews.comSupport strong local journalism and unlock premium perks: knoxnews.com/subscribe",
